## DESCRIPTION
## Conversion of a linear optimization problem to standard maximum form.
## ENDDESCRIPTION


## DBsubject(Operations research)
## DBchapter(Linear programming)
## DBsection(Constrained optimization - planar)
## Date(September 5, 2008)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## KEYWORDS('linear optimization', 'non-standard form')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',             'PGML.pl',
    'scaffold.pl',               'parserRadioButtons.pl',
    'parserRadioMultiAnswer.pl', 'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('Numeric')->variables->are(
    x1 => 'Real',
    x2 => 'Real',
    y1 => 'Real',
    y2 => 'Real',
    y3 => 'Real'
);
Context()->noreduce('(-x)-y', '(-x)+y');

@c     = (random(1, 7), random(1, 7));
@a     = (random(1, 7), random(1, 7));
$obj   = Formula("$c[0] x1 + $c[1] x2")->reduce;
$line1 = Formula("$a[0] x1 + $a[1] x2")->reduce;
$b1    = random(2, 4) * $a[0] * $a[1];
$b2    = random(4, 8);

$x1conv = Formula("y1-y2");
$x2conv = Formula("$b2-y3");

$new_obj   = Formula("-$c[0](y1-y2) - $c[1] ($b2-y3)")->reduce;
$new_line1 = Formula("-$a[0]y1 + $a[0] y2 + $a[1] y3")->reduce;
$b1_star   = Compute(-$b1 + $a[1] * $b2)->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML
Consider the following linear optimization problem:

Minimize [`w=[$obj]`] subject to

[```
\begin{aligned}
[$line1] &\geq [$b1] \\
x_2 & \leq [$b2]
\end{aligned}
```]

This problem will be converted into a problem of standard maximum form.


**Note:** enter variables in this problem as [`x_1`] as [|x1|] and [`x_2`] as [|x2|].


END_PGML
Scaffold::Begin();
Section::Begin('The Objective Function');

$rma = RadioMultiAnswer(
    [
        ['The objective function is in the correct form.'],
        [
            'The objective function needs to rewritten to \(z=\) %s',
            -1 * $obj
        ]
    ],
    1
);

BEGIN_PGML
In order for this problem to be in standard maximum form: 

[_]{$rma}
END_PGML

$obj2 = Compute("-$obj")->reduce;
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Because the original problem is a minimum problem, we write [` z= - w`] or in this case 
[`z =  [$obj2] `]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

Section::End();
Section::Begin('Types of Variables');

$x1_type = RadioButtons([ 'nonnegative', 'constrained', 'free' ],
    2, separator => '&nbsp;&nbsp;');
$x2_type = RadioButtons([ 'nonnegative', 'constrained', 'free' ],
    1, separator => '&nbsp;&nbsp;');

BEGIN_PGML
Determine the type of each of these variables:

- [`x_1`] [_]{$x1_type}
- [`x_2`] [_]{$x2_type}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
- Because there is no inequality with this variable directly, this is a *free* variable.
- The second constraint has an [`x_2`], and it is not of the form [`x_2 \geq 0`], therefore
it is *constrained*.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

Section::End();

Section::Begin('Converting Variables');

BEGIN_PGML
From the previous section, you correctly selected that [`x_1`] is free and [`x_2`] is constrained.  We will use [`y_1 = x_1^+`], [`y_2 = x_1^-`] and [`y_3=x_2'`]. For this, convert [`x_1`] and [`x_2`] to functions of [`y_1, y_2`] and [`y_3`]. 

- [`x_1= \;`] [_]{$x1conv}
- [`x_2= \;`] [_]{$x2conv}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
- To convert a free variable to a nonnegative one, use [`x_1 = x_1^+ - x_1^- = y_1 - y_2`].
- To convert a constrained variable of the form [`x_2 \leq a`], use [`x_2' = a - x_2`].  Solving for [`x_2`], one gets [`x_2 = a - x_2`] or in this case [`x_2 = [$x2conv]`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION
Section::End();

Section::Begin('Rewrite the Linear Optimization Problem');

BEGIN_PGML
For the last step, we convert the objective function and constraints to functions in terms of [`y_1, y_2`] and [`y_3.`]  Note, the 2nd constraint given converts to a nonnegative constraint.

Maximize [`z = \;`] [_]{$new_obj}  
subject to 
- [_]{$new_line1} [` \leq `] [_]{$b1_star}
- [`y_1 \geq 0, \; y_2 \geq 0, \; y_3 \geq 0`]

For the two inequalities, there should be no constants on the left side and only constants on the right side.
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Substitute the new variables in the previous section into the linear objective problem or 

Maximize [` z = [$new_obj] `]  
subject to 
- [` [$new_line1] \leq [$b1_star] `]
- [`y_1 \geq 0, \; y_2 \geq 0, \; y_3 \geq 0`]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
Section::End();

Scaffold::End();

ENDDOCUMENT();
